The SQL NSS database (cert9.db/key4.db), CA files and client certificate are
generated from the checked-in certificate/key specifications:

  ./mach python build/pgo/genpgocert.py

OpenSSL must be on PATH, or passed with --openssl. Generation uses the built
certutil/pk12util and the existing pycert/pykey test generators. All private
keys here are public test fixtures. Production NSS remains SQL-only.

To validate generated fixtures without replacing these files, use:

  ./mach python build/pgo/genpgocert.py --output-dir <object-directory>/certs

The generator derives the default server's SANs from server-locations.txt.
Add a .certspec to introduce a custom certificate. Add a matching .ca.keyspec,
.client.keyspec or .server.keyspec when it needs a different key or an export
for browser-profile import. These specifications come from Firefox ESR52.

CA validity is fixed by its specification. Other certificates default to
pycert's validity window around the generation year, except deliberately
expired fixtures. Regenerate them before that window expires and commit the
SQL databases, .ca files and .client file together. Do not commit pkcs11.txt:
it contains machine-specific module configuration and is not required here.
